{{- /*
sudo-elevation-guard.sh.tmpl — SUDO array + an elevation ladder that FAILS when
no rung succeeds. Replaces sudo-skip-guard.sh.tmpl and sudo-require-guard.sh.tmpl.

THE LADDER, in order. Only the fourth rung is new:

  1  already root                     SUDO=()
  2  sudo succeeds without prompting  SUDO=(sudo)      cached ticket, or NOPASSWD
  3  a TTY is present                 SUDO=(sudo)      sudo prompts on the terminal
  4  a usable desktop askpass helper  SUDO=(sudo -A)   sudo prompts through the session
     none of the above                declared exit 1

Rungs 2 to 4 sit behind `command -v sudo`. Without the binary, `sudo -n true`
fails for the wrong reason, and rung 3 would then read a bare TTY as success and
hand the script a SUDO array its first privileged call cannot honour. The retired
sudo-require-guard made that check; the ladder keeps it.

THE ASKPASS RUNG IS FEDORA-SHAPED. Both helper literals are Fedora paths, and only
the Fedora branch of 30-components/60-desktop-ime installs the packages. On a
Debian or Ubuntu host the rung is therefore inert — `-x` fails and the ladder
falls through to rung 3 or the declared exit, exactly as it does on a host with no
desktop. That is a narrower capability, not a regression: those hosts got no
askpass rung before this guard either. Extending it needs the Debian package name
and its own binary path (`/usr/lib/openssh/gnome-ssh-askpass`, unverified here),
so it is left out rather than guessed.

WHY THE SKIP BECAME A FAILURE. The old sudo-skip-guard exited 0 so that `chezmoi
apply` would not abort every later script — and chezmoi therefore recorded twelve
unprovisioned scripts as SUCCESSFUL runs. An unprovisioned host became
indistinguishable from a provisioned one, and recovery depended on a `sudo-usable`
capability token flipping to change each consumer's rendered content. The exit is
now skip.sh.tmpl's `transient-tolerable` direction: it exits 1, so chezmoi does not
record the run and the next apply retries it by itself. No probe, no fingerprint.

That direction's own contract note says it is valid only when nothing later depends
on the script, which is NOT true here — these are 30-linux/ and 30-components/
consumers with later phases behind them. The exception is deliberate and recorded in
skip.sh.tmpl: aborting is the correct outcome when a host that needs elevation
cannot get it. See the plan under docs/plans/ for the settled decision.

THIS REPOSITORY NEVER SUPPLIES THE PASSWORD. `SUDO_ASKPASS` names the desktop's own
askpass program, which is already wired into the session credential store
(ksshaskpass -> KWallet, gnome-ssh-askpass -> Secret Service). Nothing here prints,
stores, reads or defaults a password, and none ever reaches a process argument or a
log.

WHY THE HELPER IS A RENDER-TIME LITERAL. The path is resolved here from the
`desktop` fact, the same way dot_config/environment.d/70-desktop.conf.tmpl resolves
PINENTRY_KDE_USE_WALLET. It DIVERGES from that file's convention for `none`: there,
`none` renders the KDE variant to keep artifacts deterministic. Here `none` renders
NO helper and no rung at all, because a SUDO_ASKPASS pointing at an absent binary is
a new failure surface rather than an inert one. Resolving at render time also keeps
this partial free of a FACT_DESKTOP branch, which would need facts-sh.tmpl a second
time in the ten consumers that already inline it through headless-guard.

WHY THE GRAPHICAL CHECK IS A RUNTIME TEST. capabilities.tmpl states that a
capability never gates anything and is only ever a fingerprint input, so the display
test cannot be a probe. It mirrors the hook's own `graphical-session` resolver in
shell instead.

WHY PRIMING, AND WHAT IS BOUNDED. `timeout` wrapped around each "${SUDO[@]}" call
would kill a long `dnf install`, so the bound sits on ONE priming `sudo -A -v` that
resolves the ladder. `-A` then stays on the array: a timestamp expiry mid-run
re-prompts through the helper rather than blocking on a terminal read sudo cannot
perform. That later dialog is bounded by sudo's own passwd_timeout, not by this
guard.

ONE DIALOG PER SCRIPT, NOT PER APPLY. sudoers(5) defaults timestamp_type to `tty`,
which behaves as `ppid` when there is no terminal, and every chezmoi script is a
separate parent process. This repository sets no timestamp_type, so a TTY-less apply
authenticates in each sudo-conditioned script. That is the accepted cost of a
TTY-less run, not a defect to fix by widening the ticket.

THE DECLARED EXIT SITS BEHIND ONE CONDITION IN EVERY RENDERING. The three shapes
(kde / gnome / none) all reach it through the identical
`if [[ "$sudo_elevation_ready" -ne 1 ]]` line, so .ci/skip-declaration-site-matrix.yaml
stores ONE predicate that holds whatever desktop the render host happens to have.
Without that, the CI oracle would pass on a KDE workstation and fail on a runner.

ARGUMENT — a dict, NOT a bare string. This partial reads facts.tmpl, and a partial's
`.` is only ever what its caller passed, so the call site must hand over the chezmoi
context alongside the consumer's name:

  {{ includeTemplate "sudo-elevation-guard.sh.tmpl" (dict "ctx" . "name" "my-script") }}
*/ -}}
{{- if not (kindIs "map" .) -}}
{{-   fail (printf "sudo-elevation-guard.sh.tmpl: expected a dict, got a %s (%v). This guard reads facts.tmpl, which needs the chezmoi context, so the call site must pass it: {{ includeTemplate \"sudo-elevation-guard.sh.tmpl\" (dict \"ctx\" . \"name\" %q) }}" (kindOf .) . .) -}}
{{- end -}}
{{- if not (hasKey . "ctx") -}}
{{-   fail "sudo-elevation-guard.sh.tmpl: missing \"ctx\". Pass the chezmoi context: (dict \"ctx\" . \"name\" \"my-script\")" -}}
{{- end -}}
{{- if not (hasKey . "name") -}}
{{-   fail "sudo-elevation-guard.sh.tmpl: missing \"name\". It is half of the stable identity dotfiles-skips reports on: (dict \"ctx\" . \"name\" \"my-script\")" -}}
{{- end -}}
{{- $name := .name -}}
{{- $facts := includeTemplate "facts.tmpl" .ctx | fromYaml -}}
{{- /* The helper map is closed and both paths are root-owned /usr locations shipped
   by a distribution package, so the literal below is never attacker-influenced.
   `none` deliberately yields the empty string, which renders the rung away. */ -}}
{{- $helper := "" -}}
{{- if eq $facts.desktop "kde" -}}
{{-   $helper = "/usr/bin/ksshaskpass" -}}
{{- else if eq $facts.desktop "gnome" -}}
{{-   $helper = "/usr/libexec/openssh/gnome-ssh-askpass" -}}
{{- end -}}
# Elevation ladder; see .chezmoitemplates/sudo-elevation-guard.sh.tmpl for the
# full rationale. Every rung is a RUNTIME test: the host may gain or lose a
# credential between the render and the run.
if [[ "${EUID}" -eq 0 ]]; then
  SUDO=()
else
  SUDO=(sudo)
  sudo_elevation_ready=0
  # The binary is the precondition every later rung shares, and it is checked
  # first because a missing sudo makes `sudo -n true` fail for the WRONG reason:
  # the ladder would then read a TTY as success and hand the script a SUDO array
  # whose first privileged call dies with `command not found` instead of the
  # declared exit below. The retired sudo-require-guard checked this; keep it.
  if command -v sudo >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    # `sudo -n true` succeeds on a cached ticket or a NOPASSWD rule. `[[ -t 0 ]]`
    # is true only when stdin is a TTY, which is how sudo reads a password
    # normally; chezmoi connects stdin to its own under an interactive apply and
    # not under an agent or CI run.
    if sudo -n true 2>/dev/null || [[ -t 0 ]]; then
      sudo_elevation_ready=1
{{- if ne $helper "" }}
    else
      # No terminal to prompt on, but this host has a desktop askpass helper.
      # sudo reads the password through it and stores nothing here.
      SUDO_ASKPASS='{{ $helper }}'
      export SUDO_ASKPASS
      if [[ -x "$SUDO_ASKPASS" && -n "${WAYLAND_DISPLAY:-}${DISPLAY:-}" ]]; then
        # One bounded dialog resolves the ladder; -k follows the TERM with a KILL
        # so a cancelled prompt cannot linger. timeout signals the child's process
        # group, so the helper goes with sudo.
        if timeout -k 5 120 sudo -A -v 2>/dev/null; then
          SUDO=(sudo -A)
          sudo_elevation_ready=1
        fi
      fi
{{- end }}
    fi
  fi
  if [[ "$sudo_elevation_ready" -ne 1 ]]; then
{{ includeTemplate "skip.sh.tmpl" (dict "ctx" .ctx "owner" "sudo-elevation-guard/no-elevation-path" "form" "skip_here" "script" $name "site" "no-elevation-path" "direction" "transient-tolerable" "reason" "no elevation path: not root, and sudo cannot authenticate here (missing, or it wants a password with no terminal to ask on and no usable desktop askpass helper). Give the run a terminal (ssh -t) or authenticate first with: sudo -v") | trim | indent 4 }}
  fi
fi
